*** Add File:ops/hw/gpu/README.md
+NVIDIA GPU operationalization
+
+This document describes steps to make NVIDIA GPU workloads production-ready:
+
+1. Install NVIDIA GPU Operator (recommended)
+   - Ensures drivers, device plugin, and monitoring components are installed.
+   - Example install (requires kubectl + helm):
+     helm repo add nvidia https://nvidia.github.io/gpu-operator
+     helm repo update
+     helm install --wait --generate-name nvidia/gpu-operator --namespace gpu-operator --create-namespace
+
+2. Build validated CUDA inference image
+   - Build on a builder host with docker and desired NVIDIA toolkit.
+   - Use ops/hw/gpu/build_and_push_cuda_image.sh as template.
+
+3. Create a GPU nodepool with appropriate taints/labels
+   - Use cloud-specific nodepool scripts in ops/hw/nodepools/.
+   - Label nodes (e.g., cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool=gpu-pool or gpu=true).
+
+4. Deploy aegis vLLM chart (helm/aegis-vllm) with nodeSelector and tolerations set.
+   - Tune BATCH_MAX_SIZE and BATCH_TIMEOUT_SEC via Helm values.
+
+5. Run perf & backpressure tests:
+   - ops/ci/load_test_async.py and ops/ci/backpressure_probe.py
+   - Adjust batch size and timeout based on p95/p99 latency and GPU utilization.
+
+Notes
+- Use NVIDIA GPU Operator where possible — it manages drivers, toolkit and device plugin for you.
+- For private air-gapped environments, build the CUDA image using a machine that has access to required toolchains.
+
